WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.254
National debt servicing.

00:00:02.204 --> 00:00:04.704
Taxpayer money to bondholders.

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:09.750
The federal government owes approximately one point two trillion dollars in accumulated debt.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.102
Annual interest payments on that debt exceed forty-seven billion dollars.

00:00:14.102 --> 00:00:22.374
That forty-seven billion comes from tax revenue — which as documented in the tax policy analysis is disproportionately collected from wage earners.

00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:30.204
That forty-seven billion goes to bondholders — which includes Canadian pension funds, domestic banks, and foreign institutional investors.

00:00:30.204 --> 00:00:39.727
The fiscal loop is documented: the government borrows from capital markets, spends the borrowed money, then taxes wage earners to pay interest to the capital markets it borrowed from.

00:00:39.727 --> 00:00:45.931
Every dollar of debt service is a dollar that cannot be spent on healthcare, housing, defence, or infrastructure.

00:00:45.931 --> 00:00:49.988
The national debt is the fiscal foundation of institutional capture.

00:00:49.988 --> 00:00:51.636
The fiscal loop.

00:00:51.636 --> 00:00:56.477
How public debt transfers wealth from labour to capital through three documented steps.

00:00:56.477 --> 00:00:58.613
The opportunity cost.

00:00:58.613 --> 00:01:03.022
Every dollar of debt service is a dollar not spent on public services.

00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:07.874
Forty-seven billion dollars in annual interest exceeds the entire defence budget.

00:01:07.874 --> 00:01:13.113
It exceeds all federal transfers for childcare, pharmacare, and dental care combined.

00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:19.602
It is the fourth-largest federal expenditure after elderly benefits, health transfers, and the Canada Health Transfer.

00:01:19.602 --> 00:01:31.965
If the accumulated debt did not exist, forty-seven billion dollars annually could fund the healthcare system that is being privatized, the housing that is unaffordable, the military that cannot recruit, or the infrastructure that is crumbling.

00:01:31.965 --> 00:01:35.147
The fiscal foundation of institutional capture.

00:01:35.147 --> 00:01:39.068
The national debt is not an accident or a temporary condition.

00:01:39.068 --> 00:01:43.431
It is the fiscal foundation that makes institutional capture self-sustaining.

00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:46.261
The government borrows from capital markets.

00:01:46.261 --> 00:01:50.840
Capital markets receive guaranteed interest payments funded by taxpayers.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.693
Those capital markets include the pension funds that capture every citizen financially.

00:01:55.693 --> 00:02:00.613
The tax system that funds the interest payments disproportionately burdens wage earners.

00:02:00.613 --> 00:02:06.159
The spending that created the debt often serves the institutional interests documented across this site.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:13.829
Every layer connects: the debt funds the system, the system creates the debt, and the interest transfers wealth from labour to capital in perpetuity.

00:02:13.829 --> 00:02:16.965
This is why the system cannot reform itself.

00:02:16.965 --> 00:02:25.386
Reform would reduce borrowing, which would reduce bond issuance, which would reduce pension fund returns, which would threaten the retirement savings of every Canadian.

00:02:25.386 --> 00:02:27.522
The debt is not the problem.

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:31.443
The debt is the mechanism that ensures the captured system persists.
